			<div class="content" id="post-content-2695618">
									

<p>When the procedure was first written back when the site was getting started and the &quot;how-to&quot;s were originally implemented, it was a method of including human test subjects that could be used without bringing up to much outright maliciousness in favor of justifiable moral ambiguity, while also leaving a somewhat sinister loophole. The monthly termination, in this regard. was simply the commuted sentence being fulfilled.</p>
<p>However, from a logistical standpoint and the way current trends in politics, ethics, and various prison systems are going, there just aren't enough to go around. Fewer countries even allow a death penalty to begin with than just a decade ago. It would simply be unwise to eliminate them for the sake of security when amnestics exist. The US currently only has around 2000 inmates awaiting such sentences, and with ever last one of them enlisted as D-Class it would only give about .8 D-class personnel per object.</p>
<p>Even ignoring the more sympathetic reasons most people don't like it (they feel bad that D-class are treated as disposable, which is what the D stands for in the first place). it just doesn't make sense for a budgeted government-subsidized organization on the scale of the Foundation to throw away sources simply for being violent criminals when many of them would probably be repentant and cooperative, or at the very least manageable with amnestics and behavioral reprogramming.</p>

							</div>

								
					
