					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/buttfranklin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2721810); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2721810&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606207069" alt="Buttfranklin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2721810)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/buttfranklin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2721810); return false;" >Buttfranklin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1474220617 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Sep 2016 17:43</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2631640">
									

<p>Good tone, good structure. Can't fault you there, really. However, I'm going to no-vote because I'm fundamentally uncomfortable with the idea that we should retcon away the whole concept of monthly terminations. The concept is too deeply embedded into the site to just wish away under the pretence of some memetic effect. Obviously, I understand exactly why you'd want to do that, but I believe there's a way to save the idea without scrubbing it away as some regrettable aspect of early-site culture.</p>
<p><strong>Edit:</strong> rather than reply to every mention of this comment elsewhere in the thread, I'll edit this one. I'm not uncompromisingly against the idea of articles on this site contradicting each other - generally, I don't give a hoot about it, at all. But to me, this isn't a matter of my headcanon, this is a matter of <em>actual</em> canon. The role of D-class as test subjects is referred to in, what, about half of all articles? The idea is mentioned so often, as is so fundamental to so many parts of the site, that, to me, it sets some definite limits on the wiggle room actively encouraged by this contest. From these contest entries, I'm expecting a re-imagining or a new perspective, sure, but one that still chimes with the huge preponderance of D-class-related content on-site. And I'd rather not have to take as a prerequisite that the <em>entire</em> Foundation is hopelessly cankered head-to-toe with some memetic infohazard. This entry (and others like it), with the overzealous retconning and excessive &quot;everything you thought to be true was WRONG!&quot; plot device, feel like someone trying way too hard to brush the concept of D-class testing and termination under the carpet because it's distasteful.</p>
<p>In short: the ideas are too established to mess with too much, so we just have to work around them, not chuck 'em in the garbage. I know this thinking might be a little rigid, but&#8230; think of me as a representative from the &quot;annoying pedant&quot; delegation on this site<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-940678-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-940678-1')">1</a></sup>. (I also actively believe that the immersiveness of the site improves with a certain level of canon-building, but that's a whole other debate.)</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-940678-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-940678-1')">1</a>. There are dozens of us! Dozens!</div>
</div>

							</div>

								
